/**
 * @defgroup RoModular_Ecosystem_DevelopmentFlow Workspace, repository and delivery map
 * @ingroup RoModular_Ecosystem
 * @brief A physical map of the workspace first; build and release flow second.
 *
 * This is the page to use when the question is "where am I?" rather than
 * "which command do I run?". It starts with the repositories on disk, then
 * shows what is inside a library repository, its C++ dependencies, and finally
 * the route taken by a change.
 *
 * @section RoModularWorkspaceLayout The workspace is a set of sibling repositories
 *
 * The workspace bootstrap manifest creates this layout:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * RoModularWorkspace/
 * |
 * +-- RoModular/          ecosystem portal: catalog, architecture, bootstrap pins
 * +-- RoModularBuild/     shared CMake presets, toolchains, scripts and CI actions
 * |
 * +-- CPSTL/              portable C++ utility vocabulary
 * +-- Foundation/         general-purpose base library
 * +-- DspCore/            DSP algorithms and signal-processing types
 * +-- MCC/                music-composition theory and utilities
 * +-- MIDILAR/            MIDI library built on the lower-level libraries
 * @endcode
 *
 * These entries come from RoModular's workspace manifest. They are peer Git
 * repositories: none is a directory *owned by* another library. `RoModular/`
 * is the map and integration point; it is not the source tree for DspCore,
 * MIDILAR or the other libraries.
 *
 * contracts and reusable guidance. RoModular uses that guidance when it is
 * available, but the workspace bootstrap does not provision it as a product
 * dependency. A second Git worktree, such as `DspCore-main/`, is likewise a
 * local Git convenience rather than another architectural component.
 *
 * @section RoModularWorkspaceRoles Role of each repository
 *
 * | Repository | Role |
 * | ---------- | ---- |
 * | RoModular | Ecosystem portal: documentation, repository catalog, workspace bootstrap and release pins |
 * | RoModularBuild | Reusable build mechanics: toolchains, presets, helper scripts and composite Actions |
 * | CPSTL | Portable low-level C++ facilities |
 * | Foundation | Cross-domain general facilities |
 * | DspCore | Signal algorithms, filters, analysis, transforms, generators and lookup tables |
 * | MCC | Music-composition theory and related utilities |
 * | MIDILAR | MIDI behavior and integration on top of its declared dependencies |
 *
 * An application such as `DspCoreJuceStarter/` lives outside this library
 * workspace. It is a consumer: it may link DspCore and render a JUCE UI, but
 * its playback code, visuals and project configuration do not belong in
 * DspCore.
 *
 * @section RoModularWorkspaceInsideLibrary One library repository, concretely
 *
 * DspCore is the useful reference shape for a library checkout:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * DspCore/
 * |
 * +-- src/                shipped headers and implementation
 * +-- tests/              automated API and behavior verification
 * +-- examples/           small consumer programs; not the library itself
 * +-- docs/               Doxygen pages and API documentation inputs
 * +-- cmake/              project-specific CMake integration and adapters
 * +-- scripts/            project-level build, test and package entry points
 * +-- .github/workflows/  DspCore's CI and publication decisions
 * +-- tools/RoModularBuild/
 * |                       pinned submodule with shared build implementation
 * +-- CMakeLists.txt, CMakePresets.json, library.properties, README.md
 *                         package contract and developer entry points
 * @endcode
 *
 * The `tools/RoModularBuild/` directory is deliberately duplicated as a
 * *pinned submodule* inside a standalone library clone. It is the same build
 * project as the workspace-level `RoModularBuild/`, frozen at the revision that
 * the library has validated. It does not make build tooling part of DspCore's
 * DSP API, nor does it mean GitHub Actions live there.
 *
 * @section RoModularWorkspaceDependencies The C++ dependency graph
 *
 * This graph shows only *direct library* dependencies. `A -> B` means that
 * `A` depends on `B`; a transitive dependency follows a path, such as
 * `DspCore -> Foundation -> CPSTL`. The graph deliberately excludes the
 * library dependency.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph RoModularLibraryDependencies {
 *   graph [rankdir=LR, bgcolor="transparent", pad="0.25", nodesep="0.5", ranksep="0.75"];
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", color="#39557a", fillcolor="#eef4ff", fontname="Helvetica"];
 *   edge [color="#52677f", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *
 *   midilar [label="MIDILAR\nMIDI and music systems", fillcolor="#dce9ff"];
 *   dsp [label="DspCore\nsignal processing"];
 *   mcc [label="MCC\nmusic theory"];
 *   foundation [label="Foundation\ngeneral C++ base"];
 *   cpstl [label="CPSTL\nportable std vocabulary"];
 *
 *   midilar -> dsp;
 *   midilar -> mcc;
 *   midilar -> foundation;
 *   dsp -> foundation;
 *   mcc -> foundation;
 *   foundation -> cpstl;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * So a DSP algorithm belongs in DspCore; MIDI behavior belongs in MIDILAR;
 * build behavior shared among projects belongs in RoModularBuild; and a map or
 * release pin belongs here in RoModular.
 *
 * @section RoModularDeliveryFlow From source tree to published artifact
 *
 * Once the repository boundary is clear, delivery is straightforward:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * source + tests in the owning library
 *                 |
 *                 v
 * that library's .github/workflows/ chooses what to validate
 *                 |
 *                 v
 * pinned RoModularBuild action/preset supplies reusable mechanics
 *                 |
 *                 v
 * library commit and version tag publish the resolved dependency version
 *                 |
 *                 v
 * consumers update their required version; RoModular updates a pin if needed
 * @endcode
 *
 * Workflows are always declared in the owning repository under
 * `.github/workflows/`: for example, DspCore's Actions page lists DspCore
 * workflows. Those workflow files can call local paths such as
 * `./tools/RoModularBuild/actions/project-workflow`, which is why reusable
 * action code is found under the pinned build submodule.
 *
 * @subsection RoModularWorkspaceDspCoreRelease Example: a DspCore release
 *
 * A new DspCore API is implemented, tested and documented in `DspCore/`; its
 * supported build/package checks run from that repository. After it is tagged,
 * a consumer such as MIDILAR records the version it requires. RoModular changes
 * only if its installation manifest or high-level documentation needs the new
 * released pin. A JUCE test app can then consume the tag without adding its UI
 * or transport code to DspCore.
 *
 * @section RoModularWorkspaceOrientation A quick way to orient yourself
 *
 * 1. Start with the affected behavior and select its owning library from the
 *    workspace tree.
 * 2. Inside that repository, use `src/` for product code and `tests/` to prove
 *    it; use `docs/` to describe its contract.
 * 3. Enter `tools/RoModularBuild/` only when the shared build mechanics need
 *    changing. Read a workflow in `.github/workflows/` when you need to know
 *    what that particular project runs.
 * 4. Return to RoModular only for cross-repository catalog, bootstrap, pin or
 *    architectural documentation work.
 *
 * This separation is intentional: you should be able to understand a library
 * and make a focused library change without first understanding all of the
 * ecosystem automation.
 */
